// scrml.dev /reference/elements/db
//
// Reference page for the <db> element — the database binding state block.
//
// SPEC anchors: §4.2 (state-block classification) · §8 (SQL context) ·
// §44.7 (connection resolution) · §14.8 (protected origins) · §34 catalogue.
//
// Written 2026-07-26. The worked example below was compiled against the
// linked compiler (exit 0) before being published.

<page>

    // REFERENCE SIDEBAR opt-in — static, page-scoped, zero JS. The nav is
    // authored once in app.scrml and hidden by default; this shows it and
    // marks THIS page's link active. See scripts/gen-reference-nav.mjs.
    #{
        /* !important is load-order, not laziness: the shell's app.css is linked
           AFTER this page's stylesheet, so its `.refnav{display:none}` default
           wins at equal specificity. */
        .ref-shell { display: grid !important; }
        .refnav { display: block !important; }
        .refnav-link[data-ref="elements/db"] {
            color: rgb(125 211 252);
            background-color: rgb(14 165 233 / 0.10);
            border-left-color: rgb(56 189 248);
            font-weight: 600;
        }
    }

    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-3xl">

        <header class="not-prose mb-8 pb-6 border-b border-slate-200">
            <div class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-500 mb-2">
                Reference &rsaquo; Elements
            </div>
            <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 font-mono">
                &lt;db&gt;
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 mt-2">
                Database binding element. The compile-time anchor that connects a
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/schema"
                   class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline font-mono">
                    &lt;schema&gt;
                </a>
                to a concrete database under
                <code class="font-mono text-base">?&#123; &hellip; &#125;</code>.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-4 text-xs">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-slate-100 text-slate-700 rounded font-semibold">
                    Built-in state type
                </span>
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-slate-100 text-slate-700 rounded">
                    Block element
                </span>
                <a href="https://github.com/bryanmaclee/scrml/blob/main/compiler/SPEC.md#8"
                   class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    SPEC §8 (normative)
                </a>
            </div>
        </header>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            What it is
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            <code>&lt;db&gt;</code> is a <strong>state block</strong>, not an
            HTML element. The opener resolves against the unified state-type
            registry at name resolution: <code>db</code> is a built-in state
            type, so <code>&lt;db &hellip;&gt;</code> opens a first-class
            context rather than emitting a tag. Its body is ordinary markup
            context with the database connection in scope throughout &mdash;
            which is what gives every <code>?&#123; &hellip; &#125;</code>
            block inside it something concrete to run against.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Attributes
        </h2>

        <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700">
            <li>
                <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">src=</code>
                &mdash; the database file this block binds to.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">tables=</code>
                &mdash; which tables the block brings into scope.
                <strong>Required.</strong> Omitting it, or giving it an empty
                value, fires <code>E-PA-005</code> from the protect analyzer.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">protect=</code>
                &mdash; names a protected column, for example
                <code>protect="password"</code>. Optional; drives the
                protected-origin analysis that keeps a protected value from
                reaching the client.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Worked example
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Compiled against the linked compiler and verified to build with
            exit 0.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>&lt;program db="./app.db" title="Posts"&gt;

    &lt;schema&gt;
        posts &#123;
            id:        integer primary key
            title:     text not null
            author_id: integer not null
        &#125;
    &lt;/&gt;

    &lt;db src="./app.db" tables="posts"&gt;
        $&#123;
            function loadPosts(authorId) &#123;
                return ?&#123;`SELECT title FROM posts WHERE author_id = $&#123;authorId&#125;`&#125;.all()
            &#125;
        &#125;
        &lt;button onclick=loadPosts(1)&gt;Load&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;

&lt;/&gt;</code></pre>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            <code>&lt;db&gt;</code> vs <code>&lt;program db=&gt;</code>
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            These are two different things, and an app commonly needs both
            pointing at the same file.
            <code>&lt;program db="&hellip;"&gt;</code> tells the compiler which
            database
            <a hard href="/reference/elements/schema" class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">&lt;schema&gt;</a>
            should migrate &mdash; it is the migration target.
            <code>&lt;db src="&hellip;"&gt;</code> establishes the SQL
            <em>context</em> that <code>?&#123; &hellip; &#125;</code> blocks
            execute in. A <code>&lt;schema&gt;</code> requires the former; a
            query requires the latter. Declaring the schema but forgetting
            <code>db=</code> on <code>&lt;program&gt;</code> leaves the write
            with no database, and the row is silently dropped.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Placement and server inference
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            A function whose body contains a <code>?&#123; &hellip; &#125;</code>
            query is escalated to the server by placement inference &mdash; SQL
            is one of the escalation triggers. You do not annotate this, and you
            should not try to. The consequence worth knowing is that such a
            function is no longer client-side, so it cannot assign to a reactive
            cell. That is
            <a hard href="/reference/errors/E-RI-002" class="text-rose-700 hover:text-rose-900 underline">E-RI-002</a>.
            Return the value and let a client-side caller write the cell.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Related
        </h2>

        <ul class="space-y-1 text-slate-700">
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/schema" class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">&lt;schema&gt;</a>
                &mdash; declares the shape <code>scrml migrate</code> creates.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/contexts/sql" class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">SQL context</a>
                &mdash; what <code>?&#123; &hellip; &#125;</code> accepts and returns.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/errors/E-RI-002" class="text-rose-700 hover:text-rose-900 underline">E-RI-002</a>
                &mdash; a server-escalated function assigning a reactive cell.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/errors/E-SQL-006" class="text-rose-700 hover:text-rose-900 underline">E-SQL-006</a>
                &mdash; <code>.prepare()</code> is removed in Bun.SQL.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Specification
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            Normative text: <strong>§4.2</strong> (state-block classification
            and the deprecated whitespace opener form), <strong>§8</strong>
            (SQL context and the query API), <strong>§44.7</strong>
            (connection resolution), <strong>§14.8</strong> (protected
            origins). Spec lives at
            <a href="https://github.com/bryanmaclee/scrml/blob/main/compiler/SPEC.md"
               class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                compiler/SPEC.md
            </a>.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-10 text-sm">
            <a hard href="/reference" class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                &larr; Reference
            </a>
        </p>

    </article>

</>
